{
  "title": "How Compound Interest Works",
  "screens": [
    {
      "title": "A balance starts growing",
      "text": "Imagine you put $100 in a savings account. The bank may pay you interest: money added for keeping your balance there. At first, that extra money is small.",
      "imagePrompt": "A savings account balance showing $100, with a small amount of interest being added beside it."
    },
    {
      "title": "Interest joins the balance",
      "text": "When interest is added to your account, it becomes part of your balance. That larger balance is the new starting point for the next round.",
      "imagePrompt": "A balance made of an original deposit plus a separate piece of interest, becoming one larger balance."
    },
    {
      "title": "Interest earns interest",
      "text": "Say your $100 earns 10% interest in a year. It gains $10, making $110. The next year, 10% of $110 is $11. You earned interest on the first year's interest, too.",
      "imagePrompt": "Two yearly steps: $100 grows by $10 to $110, then the next year's interest is shown as $11."
    },
    {
      "title": "Time adds more rounds",
      "text": "The longer money stays in an account that earns interest, the more chances it has to grow on itself. That’s why starting earlier can matter, even when you add only a little at a time.",
      "imagePrompt": "Two savings balances growing in steps, with the one that starts earlier passing through more steps."
    },
    {
      "title": "Debt can grow this way",
      "text": "Compounding can work against you, too. If interest is added to an unpaid credit card balance, later interest may apply to that larger amount. Rates and timing vary, so check your account terms.",
      "imagePrompt": "An unpaid credit card balance with interest added to it, forming a larger balance for the next round."
    }
  ],
  "check": {
    "question": "Noah's savings account adds interest to his balance each year. What can happen the following year?",
